'''''Leucanthemum'' × ''superbum''''', the '''Shasta daisy''', is a commonly grown<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.gardenia.net/plant-variety/leucanthemum-x-superbum-shasta-daisy |title=Leucanthemum × superbum (Shasta daisy) |publisher=Gardenia |accessdate=23 November 2019 |archive-date=22 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200922080220/https://www.gardenia.net/plant-variety/leucanthemum-x-superbum-shasta-daisy |url-status=live }}</ref> flowering [[herbaceous]] [[perennial plant]] with the classic daisy appearance of white petals (ray florets) around a yellow disc, similar to the oxeye daisy ''[[Leucanthemum vulgare]]'' <small>Lam,</small> but larger.

It originated as a hybrid produced in 1890 by the American horticulturist [[Luther Burbank]] from a number of daisies. First, he crossed ''Leucanthemum vulgare'' with ''[[Leucanthemum maximum]]''; this double hybrid was itself crossed with ''[[Leucanthemum lacustre]]''.<ref name=Clausen>Ruth Rogers Clausen and Thomas Christopher. ''Essential Perennials: The Complete Reference to 2700 Perennials for the Home Garden.'' Timber Press, 2015. p. 250. {{ISBN|9781604696721}}</ref><ref name=Gilmer>Maureen Gilmer. [https://www.thedailyjournal.com/story/life/2014/08/15/discovering-daisy/14104057/ "Discovering a Daisy".] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422061018/https://www.thedailyjournal.com/story/life/2014/08/15/discovering-daisy/14104057/ |date=2022-04-22 }} ''The Daily Journal''. 15 August 2014. Retrieved 15 April 2015.</ref> The resulting ''Leucanthemum'' triple hybrid was crossed with ''[[Nipponanthemum nipponicum]]'', creating an [[intergeneric hybrid|intergeneric cross]] of species from three continents.<ref name=Clausen/><ref name=Gilmer/> It was named after [[Mount Shasta]], because its petals were the color of the snow. Some members of the genus are considered [[noxious weed]]s, but the Shasta daisy remains a favorite garden plant.

Many [[cultivar]]s are suitable for [[cut flowers]], such as 'Becky', 'Esther Read', 'Silberprinzesschen' ({{tdes|Silver Princess}}), 'Snow Lady', 'Tinkerbell', 'Wirral Pride', 'Wirral Supreme'. The cultivars 'T.E. Killin'<ref>{{cite web |title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Leucanthemum'' × ''superbum'' 'T.E. Killin' |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/63102/Leucanthemum-x-superbum-T-E-Killin-(d)/Details |accessdate=2 October 2020 |archive-date=5 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210505165141/https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/63102/Leucanthemum-x-superbum-T-E-Killin-(d)/Details |url-status=live }}</ref> and 'Wirral Supreme' <ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/94524/Leucanthemum-x-superbum-Wirral-Supreme-(d)/Details |title=''Leucanthemum'' × ''superbum'' 'Wirral Supreme' |publisher=RHS |accessdate=1 October 2020 |archive-date=22 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422061021/https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/94524/leucanthemum-x-superbum-wirral-supreme-(d)/details |url-status=live }}</ref> have gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf |title=AGM Plants - Ornamental |date=July 2017 |page=59 |publisher=Royal Horticultural Society |accessdate=21 March 2018 |archive-date=5 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180105180412/https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

